Define steepest ascent properly: maximise $df = \langle 
abla f, hangle$ subject to $\|h\|_{M} = 1$ where $\|h\|_{M}^{2} = h^{T} M h$. Substituting $u = M^{1/2}h$ turns it into maximising $\langle M^{-1/2} 
abla f, uangle$ over unit u, whose maximiser is u proportional to $M^{-1/2} 
abla f$, i.e. h proportional to $M^{-1} 
abla f$. With $M = I$ this is the ordinary gradient; with M = Fisher information it is the natural gradient, which is invariant to reparameterisation of the model's distribution; with M diagonal it resembles what Adam does. So 'downhill' is a metric-dependent claim, and the choice of metric is a real modelling decision hiding inside your optimizer.
